<section id="correlation_plot">
    <h1 id="hd_id941791901764252"><variable id="h1"><link href="text/schart/01/type_correlation_circle.xhp">Correlation Circle Plot</link></variable></h1>
    <description id="par_id431791901764252">A correlation circle plot (or correlation circle) is a visualization used in <link href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Principal_component_analysis">Principal Component Analysis (PCA)</link> to show how the original variables of a dataset relate to the principal components — and to each other.</description>
</section>
<h2 id="hd_id941791919810223">Correlation Circle</h2>
<paragraph role="paragraph" id="par_id481791910178474">A Correlation Circle chart is a circle of radius 1 where:</paragraph>
<list type="unordered">
    <listitem>
        <paragraph id="par_id811791908810620"  role="listitem">Each original variable (feature) is drawn as a vector (arrow) from the origin.</paragraph>
    </listitem>
    <listitem>
        <paragraph id="par_id301792156609708"  role="listitem">The X-coordinate value is the correlation of that series' feature column with the <emph>first</emph> dimension column.</paragraph>
    </listitem>
    <listitem>
        <paragraph id="par_id31792156616019"  role="listitem">The Y-coordinate value is the correlation of that series' feature column with the <emph>second</emph> dimension column.</paragraph>
    </listitem>
</list>

<h2 id="hd_id351792015850281">Correlation Circle Data Range</h2>

<paragraph role="paragraph" id="par_id441792015934001">Data for the correlation circle plot must be arranged so that the last two columns are the PCA dimensions and every preceding column is a feature.</paragraph>

<note id="par_id811792015991817">The PCA data depends on the feature values. However, you must provide the PCA data yourself, as %PRODUCTNAME has no built-in function to compute it.</note>

<h3 id="hd_id141792155215699">Example</h3>
<paragraph role="paragraph" id="par_id591792154759217">
    <image src="media/helpimg/schart/correlation_circle_table.avif" id="img_id221792154759217" width="580px" height="155px"><alt id="alt_id231792154759217">Correlation circle chart data</alt></image>
</paragraph>

<paragraph role="paragraph" id="par_id581792156770827">Error bars, trend lines and area properties are turned off for this chart type, since a point stands for a worked out pair rather than for a value in a cell, and symbols are turned on because the tip of each arrow carries one.</paragraph>

<paragraph role="paragraph" id="par_id731792156776759">The arrows take the line colour and width of their series, and line thickness is disregarded, which is what keeps a crowd of them apart.</paragraph>

<paragraph role="paragraph" id="par_id801792154968866">
    <image src="media/helpimg/schart/correlation_circle_chart.webp" id="img_id761792154968866"  width="423px" height="349px"><alt id="alt_id941792154968866">Correlation circle plot</alt></image>
</paragraph>

<paragraph role="paragraph" id="par_id741792156783223">The legend hands out the same symbol as the arrow tip, so a key can be matched to an arrow.</paragraph>

<paragraph role="paragraph" id="par_id201792156789299">Grids are turned off by default for this chart as the matter is in the direction, angles and magnitude of the rays from the origin.</paragraph>

<section id="relatedtopics">
    <paragraph role="paragraph" id="par_id531792156962864"><link href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Principal_component_analysis">Principal Component Analysis (PCA) in Wikipedia</link></paragraph>
</section>

